Another (admittedly pretty small) benefit for not rubbing magic lamps is
that the chance of getting a djinni from a smoky potion depends on how
many djinni have been generated, including from rubbing magic lamps.  So
if you rub a magic lamp, and then later quaff a smoky potion, you're less
likely to get two wishes than if you had waited on rubbing the lamp until
after quaffing the smoky potion.

Speaking for myself, I decide whether or not to rub a magic lamp based on
the circumstances.  An early magic lamp, say from Izchak's store, I would
probably rub for DSM, unless I already had DSM or other good armor. 
Once past the early game I'd probably keep it for a light source unless
there was something I really wanted to wish for.  I also keep the smoky
potion I mentioned above in mind: in my current game, smoky potions are
full healing, so I won't be rubbing any magic lamps until after I'm
finished w/ alchemy.


